⚡ Execution & Comms Agent — Full Responsibility Breakdown

1. Workflow Reconfiguration
When the business objective changes (e.g., clearance mode), it's this agent that physically swaps the active workflow — not the Decision agent. It:

Saves the current workflow config to the rollback registry (Cosmos DB) before touching anything
Atomically updates the feature flag in Azure App Configuration to point to the new workflow variant
Activates or deactivates integration connectors (e.g., turns on the promo-pricing-sync connector, turns off the standard replenishment connector)

If the Decision agent decides what to do, the Execution agent decides how to wire things up to make it happen.

2. Rollback & Recovery
This is the safety escape hatch for the whole system. When something goes wrong:

Detects anomalies via Azure Monitor alerts (e.g., PO creation rate spikes to 847/hr)
Triggers the circuit breaker signal (sets a flag in App Configuration that Polly picks up in the Decision agent)
Loads the last stable workflow version from the rollback registry
Flips the feature flag back — entire rollback in under 2 minutes, no redeployment
Writes the incident timeline to Azure Monitor for post-mortem

Without this agent, there's no autonomous recovery path.

3. Inter-Store Inventory Reallocation
In Scenario 1 and Scenario 3, once the Decision agent calculates the reallocation plan (which stores have surplus, which are critical), it hands the execution of that plan to this agent:

Calls the WMS Transfer Order API for each store-to-store movement
Sequences the calls correctly (you can't initiate a transfer from a store that's already being transferred to)
Handles partial failures — if 2 of 12 WMS calls fail, it retries those 2 without re-running all 12
Updates the expected inbound schedule in ERP so stock-in-transit is visible to the Sensing agent

This is real operational work, not messaging.

4. Supplier & Connector Activation
When a disruption happens (Scenario 3), this agent:

Activates the emergency-3PL connector (pre-configured but dormant) so expedited freight routing becomes available
Updates the WMS inbound receipt schedule to reflect the new supplier's lead time
Calls the EDI acknowledgement back to the supplier confirming the PO was received

It's the only agent with write access to the integration connector registry.

5. Notifications & Human-in-the-Loop
This is the "comms" part — but it's more nuanced than just sending a message:

Routine notifications: Daily sell-through digest, clearance activation summary, PO batch summary
Approval cards: When Decision agent creates a PO over the approval threshold ($80K), Execution sends a Teams Adaptive Card with the full reasoning trace attached — the buyer sees why the agent made the decision, not just what it did
P1 Incident alerts: Within 60 seconds of a supplier delay EDI arriving, on-call buyer + supply chain manager get a Teams + SMS notification with store-level impact map
Escalation routing: If a circuit breaker stays open for >10 minutes, it escalates up the chain automatically


6. Audit Logging
Every action taken by any agent is logged here — not as an afterthought but as a structured AgentDecisionEvent:
correlation_id, scenario_type, agent_name, 
action_taken, inputs_summary, confidence_score, 
duration_ms, outcome, timestamp
This goes to Azure Monitor Logs (immutable) and is the source of truth for compliance audits, post-incident reviews, and any "why did the system do that?" question from a buyer.

